					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610097697" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1505869242 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Sep 2017 01:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3597722">
									

<p>So, the new major plot points about the Ethics Committee are:<br />
- The Ethics Committee uses irc<br />
- Observers are good fiends because EC life is hard</p>
<p>I guess I understand if this is an intro to a tale series, but as a piece of narrative it accomplishes literally nothing. We don't even really get decent characterization, made worse by the fact that the names aren't easily parsed and are basically unreadable on mobile.</p>
<p>It's been said before, but I find writing dialogue in the literal form of IRC chat to be incredibly indulgent and immersion-breaking. Chat makes sense for GAW, but the idea that the Foundation's secret communication system happens to be exactly the same relatively obscure system that we use here, and that people happen to talk there in a similar manner to our chat, is just a huge leap. It's going to take more than just stating &quot;we use sirc&quot; and &quot;you don't have to be professional&quot; about three times each for me to buy that, and frankly that got a little grating given that nothing else was provided.</p>
<p>So yeah, declaring some poor intentions with almost no reasoning. I'll see how attached I get to these people further on, but this isn't a great start.<br />
-1</p>

							</div>

								
					
