															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1735419&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610097697" alt="Lt Flops" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1735419)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lt-flops" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1735419); return false;" >Lt Flops</a></span> <span class="odate time_1583689919 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Mar 2020 17:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4537663">
									

<p>I'm in agreement with some of the past critiques on this Tale, but despite seeing them as valid reasons to disapprove of the subject matter, I actually really enjoyed this one. It's informative as to the duties and personalities of each character introduced. It's cute. I dig the concept of an &quot;Observer&quot; group within the Ethics Committee; it aligns with my headcanon for how that particular department operates.</p>
<p>What works here, for me, <em>is</em> the casual nature of it. Foundation Orientations have never felt&#8230; personal? personable? to me. To be entirely fair, that often displays a mark of professionalism on the part of the Foundation, and you would expect senior personnel in charge of handling junior employee orientation to behave professionally &#8212; that's all fine and well. But that kind of demeanour doesn't always strike me as conducive to building positive professional relationships in the Foundation's context. IMO, Orientations should be two things: (1) Informative, and (2) Welcoming. They mostly succeed with the first but fall short with the second.</p>
<p>The informal way this is presented feels realistic to me. I can better relate to the characters because of it. They feel like people. All rambling aside, I like that.</p>

							</div>

